theory Resonate
begin

/*
================================================================================

  The Resonate protocol as a Tamarin state machine.

  Modelled after the Lean abstract machine in this repository's `spec/`
  (`spec/01-protocol`, `spec/02-abstract`): one rule per transition of that
  machine, with the same guards, the same state fields, and the same effects.
  On top of that faithful core this theory adds two things the specification
  does not have, and which are the subject of the analysis:

    (1) an IDENTITY AND CAPABILITY model.  Servers, workers and clients are
        principals with long-term signing keys.  Every request is signed.  A
        task is OFFERED by the server to a worker group; a worker CLAIMS it
        with `task.acquire`; on a successful claim the server mints a
        capability

            cap = sign(<'cap', S, tid, version, W>, sk(S))

        bound to that particular worker W and to that particular version.
        `task.fence`, `task.suspend`, `task.complete`, `task.release` and
        `task.heartbeat` are gated on presenting that capability AND on the
        request being signed by the worker the capability names.

    (2) END-TO-END ENCRYPTION of promise params and values.  A promise carries

            pblob = senc(param, ek)
            kblob = aenc(<'ek', pid, ek>, pk(gsk(G)))

        where `ek` is a fresh envelope key and `gsk(G)` is the private key of
        the worker group G that will execute the promise.  The server stores
        and forwards both blobs and holds no key that opens either.  Values
        are sealed under the same envelope key by the completing worker.

  ------------------------------------------------------------------------
  WHAT IS MODELLED, AND WHAT IS ABSTRACTED
  ------------------------------------------------------------------------

  Modelled, transition for transition, from `02-abstract/external.lean` and
  `02-abstract/internal.lean`:

    promise.create (all four promise kinds, plus the idempotent re-create)
    promise.settle (the `settable` guard, plus the already-settled no-op)
    promise.get
    promise.register_callback  (all branches: 400 / 404 / 422 / register / settled)
    promise.register_listener  (all branches)
    task.create
    task.acquire
    task.fence   (create and settle actions)
    task.suspend (arity 1 and arity 2, all branches incl. the 300 branch)
    task.complete  (= `taskFulfill` in the Lean spec)
    task.release
    task.halt / task.continue
    task.heartbeat
    r1  promise timeout          (timer -> resolved, otherwise -> rejectedTimedout)
    r3  listener drain           (emits `unblock`)
    r4  callback drain           (`resumeOne`)
    r5  lease timeout
    r6  retry dispatch           (emits `execute`)

  Deliberately abstracted, with the reason in each case:

    TIME.  Tamarin terms carry no order relation, so `now`, `timeoutAt`,
    `expiresAt` and `retryAt` are not represented as values.  Every step whose
    only guard is a deadline comparison -- the promise timeout (r1), the lease
    timeout (r5), the retry dispatch (r6) -- is modelled as a rule that may
    fire at ANY point at which its non-temporal guards hold.  This is a strict
    OVER-approximation: every run of the Lean machine has a corresponding run
    here, and this theory additionally admits runs in which deadlines fire
    early.  Safety properties proved here therefore hold of the real machine.
    It is not a reduction of the state space; it enlarges it.

    THE `resumes` LEDGER.  `TaskObject.resumes` gates no transition in the
    specification -- it is read only by `TaskObject.toRecord`, which reports
    its LENGTH.  Multiset rewriting cannot clear an unbounded ledger in one
    step, so resumes are recorded as `TaskResume(S, tid, ver, awaited)` facts
    tagged with the task version at which they were recorded.  Clearing on
    acquisition (which bumps the version) is therefore exact; clearing on
    suspension, which does not bump the version, leaves a residue visible
    only in the reported count.  No guard and no security property reads it.

    BIRTH PAST THE DEADLINE.  `createPromise` has a second branch: a promise
    whose `timeoutAt` has already passed is born SETTLED -- `resolved` if it
    is a timer, `rejectedTimedout` otherwise -- with its task, if it has one,
    born `fulfilled`.  With time abstracted, that state is reached here by
    creating the promise pending and firing `Internal_Promise_Timeout`
    immediately after, with no step in between that could observe the
    difference.  The branch is therefore present, as a two-rule path rather
    than a one-rule one.

    SCHEDULES.  `nextCron`, `occurrences` and `expand` are `opaque` in the
    specification -- calendar arithmetic explicitly outside the protocol, and
    carried unexercised there.  A schedule contributes exactly one thing the
    rest of the protocol can see: another way for a promise to be born, with
    a server-chosen id.  That is modelled by `Schedule_Fire` below, with
    `expand` as a free function symbol.

    SEARCH.  `promiseSearch` and `taskSearch` answer 501 in the specification.

    RESPONSE ECHOES.  A response here carries the outcome -- the status, the
    state a promise settled into -- but not a payload the requester itself
    supplied a moment earlier.  Two messages DO carry payload, because that is
    their job: the `task.acquire` response, which delivers a promise's param to
    the worker that claimed it, and the `unblock`, which delivers a value to a
    listener.  Everywhere else a payload in a response would be a term going
    straight back out the way it came in, which no principal learns anything
    from and which costs the prover an open chain apiece.

  The envelope is DOMAIN-SEPARATED: a param is sealed as
  `senc(<'param', p>, ek)` and a value as `senc(<'value', v>, ek)`.  The tags
  are not decoration.  Without them the two blobs are interchangeable, and
  `models/resonate-payload.spthy` shows what that costs: the prover finds, in
  four steps, a run in which the creator accepts its OWN PARAM back as the
  answer to its promise, replayed off the wire as an `unblock`, with no worker
  having run at all.  Sealing under one key is not enough; what is sealed has
  to say what it is.

  ------------------------------------------------------------------------
  WHY A COMPROMISED SERVER IS THE RIGHT WAY TO ASK "CAN THE SERVER READ IT?"
  ------------------------------------------------------------------------

  Everything the server ever holds -- `pblob`, `kblob`, `vblob` -- also travels
  over the public network, so the Dolev-Yao adversary already knows every term
  in the server's store.  Adding `Compromise_Agent` on the server hands the
  adversary the server's long-term key as well.  At that point the adversary
  knows strictly more than the server does.  A secrecy lemma that holds while
  `LtkReveal(S)` is permitted therefore says exactly: THE SERVER CANNOT READ
  IT.  The secrecy lemmas below do not exclude server compromise; they exclude
  only compromise of the worker group's key or of a worker in the group.

================================================================================
*/

builtins: signing, asymmetric-encryption, symmetric-encryption

functions: vsucc/1, expand/2

// ============================================================================
// SECTION 0 -- restrictions
// ============================================================================

restriction equality:
  "All x y #i. Eq(x, y) @ #i ==> x = y"

restriction inequality:
  "All x #i. Neq(x, x) @ #i ==> F"

restriction only_once:
  "All x #i #j. OnlyOnce(x) @ #i & OnlyOnce(x) @ #j ==> #i = #j"

/* `PromiseState.settable`: `pending` is not a settlement and `rejectedTimedout`
   is server-owned -- only the timeout path writes it, so a client can never
   forge one.  (`01-protocol/validation.lean`.) */
restriction settable:
  "All s #i. Settable(s) @ #i ==>
       s = 'resolved' | s = 'rejected' | s = 'rejectedCanceled'"

/* A promise id names at most one promise.  `promiseCreate` reads the store
   before writing and returns the existing record if the id is taken; the
   `*_Idempotent` rules below cover that branch, so restricting genuine births
   to one per id loses no behaviour. */
restriction unique_promise_id:
  "All s pid #i #j. PromiseBorn(s, pid) @ #i & PromiseBorn(s, pid) @ #j
       ==> #i = #j"

/* `PromiseObject.addCallback` / `addListener` are idempotent: a second
   registration of the same pair is a no-op that still answers 200.  The
   `*_Duplicate` rules cover the no-op; this keeps the ledger a set. */
restriction unique_callback:
  "All s pid aw #i #j. CallbackAdded(s, pid, aw) @ #i
                     & CallbackAdded(s, pid, aw) @ #j ==> #i = #j"

restriction unique_listener:
  "All s pid a #i #j. ListenerAdded(s, pid, a) @ #i
                    & ListenerAdded(s, pid, a) @ #j ==> #i = #j"

/* `TaskObject.resumes` is deduplicated by `resumeOne`. */
restriction unique_resume:
  "All s tid v aw #i #j. ResumeAdded(s, tid, v, aw) @ #i
                       & ResumeAdded(s, tid, v, aw) @ #j ==> #i = #j"

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   COLLAPSING IDENTICAL REPETITIONS.

   Three steps can fire again and again with no observable difference between
   one firing and the next, and each of them is a rule that CONSUMES a linear
   fact and puts back exactly what it took.  That shape is a cycle in the
   prover's backward search -- every `TaskSt` goal has the rule as one of its
   own sources -- and it is the reason the capability lemmas did not converge.

   These three restrictions keep the first firing of each and drop the
   repeats.  The argument that this loses nothing is the same in all three
   cases, and it is a statement about the model, not a hope:

     * the rule restores the linear facts it consumed, unchanged, so the store
       after the second firing is identical to the store after the first;
     * the term it sends is identical to the one the first firing sent, so the
       adversary's knowledge after is identical to its knowledge before;
     * nothing in the theory reads the fields the rule does write -- the
       outbox slot's contents are read by no rule, and `expiresAt`, which the
       heartbeat exists to move, is not represented at all.

   So for any trace containing a repeat there is a trace without it, over the
   same states, with the same adversary knowledge, and satisfying the same
   action-fact formulas.  Any attack in the first is an attack in the second.

   `Internal_Retry_Dispatch` at a NEW version is a different event and is not
   collapsed -- the restrictions are keyed on the version. -------------------- */

restriction dispatch_once_per_version:
  "All s tid v #i #j. Dispatch(s, tid, v) @ #i & Dispatch(s, tid, v) @ #j
       ==> #i = #j"

restriction heartbeat_once_per_lease:
  "All s tid v w #i #j. Heartbeat(s, tid, v, w) @ #i & Heartbeat(s, tid, v, w) @ #j
       ==> #i = #j"

restriction suspend_refusal_once_per_lease:
  "All s tid v w #i #j. SuspendRefused(s, tid, v, w) @ #i
                      & SuspendRefused(s, tid, v, w) @ #j ==> #i = #j"



/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   THE ANALYSIS CONFIGURATION, `-D=Core`.

   Twelve rules are gated behind this flag.  Unlike `-D=Reduced`, which drops
   rules that provably change nothing, this one drops rules that DO change
   things -- so a result under `-D=Core` is a statement about the sub-protocol
   that remains, not about the whole theory.  It is stated here so that what
   was verified, and over what, is not a matter of inference:

     Schedule_Create, Schedule_Fire
         A promise birth site with a server-derived id.  `nextCron` and
         `expand` are opaque in the specification; nothing else reads a
         schedule.

     Server_Task_Create, Server_Task_Create_Adopt, Worker_Task_Create,
     Worker_Start_Own
         The `task.create` family: a SECOND way to reach `acquired`, and a
         second sealed-payload delivery path.  `task.acquire` remains, and it
         is the path every capability lemma is about.  What is lost with
         these is coverage of the version-less adoption branch.

     Server_Mark_External_Timer, Internal_Promise_Timeout_Timer
         Timer promises.  A timer is never targeted, carries no task, and
         resolves at its deadline; the non-timer timeout rules remain, so the
         deadline path itself is still exercised.

     Server_Promise_Register_Callback
         Standalone callback registration.  `task.suspend` still registers
         callbacks, so the callback drain and `resumeOne` are still reached.

     Server_Task_Suspend_2, Server_Task_Suspend_2_AlreadySettled,
     Worker_Suspend_2
         Suspend at arity two.  Arity one remains, with both its branches.
         What is lost is the all-or-nothing across a multi-await suspend.

   Why: the prover's source refinement does not converge on the full theory on
   a 16 GB host.  The cost is in the sealed payload: a worker must DECONSTRUCT
   what the server passed through as an unconstrained variable, and each
   delivery path that does so opens chains.  `-D=Core` leaves exactly one such
   path -- `task.acquire` to `Worker_Start` -- plus the `unblock` that delivers
   a value to a listener, which is the least the protocol can be modelled with
   and still be the protocol.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

// ============================================================================
// SECTION 1 -- principals, keys and compromise
// ============================================================================

/* A worker group.  `gsk` is the group's decryption key: it is the ONLY key
   that opens a promise's `kblob`, and therefore the only key that reaches a
   param or a value.  It is held by every worker of the group and by nobody
   else -- in particular not by the server. */
rule Setup_Group:
    [ Fr(~gsk) ]
  --[ NewGroup($G), OnlyOnce(<'group', $G>) ]->
    [ !GroupKey($G, ~gsk), !Pk($G, pk(~gsk)), Out(pk(~gsk)) ]

rule Register_Server:
    [ Fr(~ltkS) ]
  --[ NewServer($S), OnlyOnce(<'agent', $S>) ]->
    [ !Ltk($S, ~ltkS), !Pk($S, pk(~ltkS)), !Server($S), Out(pk(~ltkS)) ]

rule Register_Worker:
    [ Fr(~ltkW), !GroupKey($G, ~gsk) ]
  --[ NewWorker($W, $G), OnlyOnce(<'agent', $W>) ]->
    [ !Ltk($W, ~ltkW), !Pk($W, pk(~ltkW))
    , !Worker($W, $G, ~gsk), !Member($W, $G)
    , Out(pk(~ltkW)) ]

rule Register_Client:
    [ Fr(~ltkC) ]
  --[ NewClient($C), OnlyOnce(<'agent', $C>) ]->
    [ !Ltk($C, ~ltkC), !Pk($C, pk(~ltkC)), !Client($C), Out(pk(~ltkC)) ]

/* Long-term key compromise.  Applies to servers, workers and clients alike.
   For a SERVER this is the "honest-but-curious / fully compromised server"
   scenario discussed in the header: the adversary already sees every blob the
   server stores, and now holds its signing key too. */
rule Compromise_Agent:
    [ !Ltk($A, ~lt) ]
  --[ LtkReveal($A) ]->
    [ Out(~lt) ]

/* A compromised worker.  It gives up its signing key AND the group
   decryption key it holds.  Because every worker of a group holds the same
   `gsk`, compromising ANY member exposes every param and every value ever
   sealed to that group -- a property of this key distribution, and one the
   secrecy lemmas below make explicit rather than hide. */
rule Compromise_Worker:
    [ !Worker($W, $G, ~gsk), !Ltk($W, ~ltkW) ]
  --[ WorkerCompromised($W, $G), GroupReveal($G), LtkReveal($W) ]->
    [ Out(~gsk), Out(~ltkW) ]

/* Compromise of a worker group's decryption key on its own -- a leaked key
   store rather than a leaked worker.  This is the only compromise that can
   open a param or a value. */
rule Compromise_Group:
    [ !GroupKey($G, ~gsk) ]
  --[ GroupReveal($G) ]->
    [ Out(~gsk) ]


// ============================================================================
// SECTION 2 -- promise birth
//
//   `AbstractModel.createPromise` (state.lean).  A promise is born pending if
//   its deadline is in the future, and born settled if it is not; a promise
//   carrying `resonate:target` is born together with a task.  The four kinds
//   below are the four tag shapes the specification distinguishes:
//
//     'targeted'  -- carries `resonate:target`; a worker owns its execution,
//                    so it is born with a pending task and an armed dispatch.
//     'ext'       -- carries `resonate:external`; settled from outside, no task.
//     'timer'     -- carries `resonate:timer`; resolves at its deadline, no task.
//     'local'     -- no tags; not `external`, so it takes no callbacks and no
//                    listeners.
//
//   The first three are `PromiseObject.external`.
// ============================================================================

/* A client creates a targeted promise.  It seals the param under a fresh
   envelope key and seals that key to the worker group's public key.  It keeps
   `ek` so that it can read the value later. */
rule Client_Create_Targeted:
let pblob = senc(<'param', ~param>, ~ek)
    kblob = aenc(<'ek', $pid, ~ek>, pkG)
    body  = <'promise.create', $S, $pid, 'targeted', $tgt, $G, pblob, kblob>
in
    [ Fr(~ek), Fr(~param)
    , !Client($C), !Ltk($C, ~ltkC), !Server($S), !Pk($G, pkG) ]
  --[ ClientCreates($C, $pid, $G)
    , SecretParam(~param, $pid, $G)
    , Seal(~ek, $pid, $G)
    , OnlyOnce(<'client-create', $C, $pid>) ]->
    [ Out(<body, sign(body, ~ltkC)>)
    , ClientAwaits($C, $S, $pid, ~ek, ~param) ]

/* A client creates an external (untargeted) promise -- the "human approval",
   "webhook", "outside event" shape.  No worker executes it; someone settles
   it with `promise.settle`, and clients learn of the settlement through
   `promise.register_listener` and the `unblock` message. */
rule Client_Create_External:
let pblob = senc(<'param', ~param>, ~ek)
    kblob = aenc(<'ek', $pid, ~ek>, pkG)
    body  = <'promise.create', $S, $pid, 'ext', 'none', $G, pblob, kblob>
in
    [ Fr(~ek), Fr(~param)
    , !Client($C), !Ltk($C, ~ltkC), !Server($S), !Pk($G, pkG) ]
  --[ ClientCreates($C, $pid, $G)
    , SecretParam(~param, $pid, $G)
    , Seal(~ek, $pid, $G)
    , OnlyOnce(<'client-create', $C, $pid>) ]->
    [ Out(<body, sign(body, ~ltkC)>)
    , ClientAwaits($C, $S, $pid, ~ek, ~param) ]

/* Server side of `promise.create` for a TARGETED promise.  `createPromise`:
   the promise is born pending, and because it carries `resonate:target` a
   task is born with it, pending, at version zero, with its dispatch armed.
   `ExecSlot` is the outbox slot keyed by task id -- `Effect.setMessage`
   replaces the entry with the same key, so a task has exactly one. */
rule Server_Promise_Create_Targeted:
let body = <'promise.create', $S, pid, 'targeted', tgt, grp, pblob, kblob>
    resp = <'promise.created', $S, pid, 'pending', 'novalue'>
in
    [ In(<body, sig>)
    , !Server($S), !Ltk($S, ~ltkS), !Pk($A, pkA) ]
  --[ Eq(verify(sig, body, pkA), true)
    , PromiseBorn($S, pid)
    , TaskBorn($S, pid, 'v0')
    , CreatedBy($S, pid, $A) ]->
    [ !PromiseMeta($S, pid, 'targeted', tgt, grp, pblob, kblob, $A)
    , !ExternalP($S, pid)
    , PromiseSt($S, pid, 'pending', 'novalue')
    , TaskSt($S, pid, 'tpending', 'v0', 'nobody')
    , ExecSlot($S, pid, 'empty')
    , Out(<resp, sign(resp, ~ltkS)>) ]

/* Server side of `promise.create` for an untargeted promise: external, timer
   or local.  `kind` is constrained by the restriction below so that this rule
   cannot be used to smuggle a targeted promise past the task birth above. */
rule Server_Promise_Create_Untargeted:
let body = <'promise.create', $S, pid, kind, 'none', grp, pblob, kblob>
    resp = <'promise.created', $S, pid, 'pending', 'novalue'>
in
    [ In(<body, sig>)
    , !Server($S), !Ltk($S, ~ltkS), !Pk($A, pkA) ]
  --[ Eq(verify(sig, body, pkA), true)
    , Untargeted(kind)
    , PromiseBorn($S, pid)
    , CreatedBy($S, pid, $A) ]->
    [ !PromiseMeta($S, pid, kind, 'none', grp, pblob, kblob, $A)
    , PromiseSt($S, pid, 'pending', 'novalue')
    , Out(<resp, sign(resp, ~ltkS)>) ]

restriction untargeted_kinds:
  "All k #i. Untargeted(k) @ #i ==> k = 'ext' | k = 'timer' | k = 'local'"

/* `PromiseObject.external`: the external tag, a target tag, or the timer tag.
   Emitted as a separate rule so that the untargeted-creation rule above stays
   one rule rather than three. */
rule Server_Mark_External_Ext:
    [ !PromiseMeta($S, pid, 'ext', 'none', grp, pblob, kblob, cr) ]
  --[ MarkExternal($S, pid), OnlyOnce(<'ext', $S, pid>) ]->
    [ !ExternalP($S, pid) ]

#ifdef Core
// Server_Mark_External_Timer: omitted under -D=Core -- see the note in section 0
#else
rule Server_Mark_External_Timer:
    [ !PromiseMeta($S, pid, 'timer', 'none', grp, pblob, kblob, cr) ]
  --[ MarkExternal($S, pid), OnlyOnce(<'ext', $S, pid>) ]->
    [ !ExternalP($S, pid) ]
#endif


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   THE RESPONSE-ONLY RULES, and why they can be switched off.

   The eight rules below share a shape: they leave the store EXACTLY as they
   found it and emit a response.  `promise.get` and `task.get` are reads; the
   `*_Idempotent`, `*_Duplicate`, `*_Settled` and `*_Noop` rules are the
   branches where the specification answers 200 with the record that is
   already there and writes nothing.

   They are part of the protocol and they are modelled.  But they cost the
   prover a great deal: each is a source for the very linear fact it consumes,
   which is a cycle in the backward search, and `promise.get` in particular
   hands back three opaque blobs, which is what opens the chains that stall
   the precomputation.

   Building with `-D=Reduced` removes them, and the reduction is SOUND for
   every property in this theory:

     (i)  they write nothing, so the reachable set of store states is
          unchanged -- delete them from any trace and the remaining steps
          still apply, to the same states, in the same order;

     (ii) they add nothing to the adversary.  Every term they emit is already
          derivable: a promise's `pblob` and `kblob` cross the network in the
          `promise.create` request that carried them, `vblob` in the
          `task.fulfill` or `promise.settle` request that carried it, and a
          task's version in the `execute` message and the claim response.  The
          one genuinely new term is the server's signature over the response,
          and no rule in the theory consumes a `promise.record`,
          `task.record`, `promise.created`, `promise.settled`,
          `promise.callback.*` or `promise.listener.*` message.  A signature
          nothing accepts extends no attack.

   So a trace of the full theory maps to a trace of the reduced one with the
   same action facts on the store-changing steps, and every lemma here is
   stated over those.  Verify under `-D=Reduced`, and read the result as a
   statement about the full theory.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
#ifdef Reduced
// Server_Promise_Create_Idempotent: response-only, omitted under -D=Reduced
#else
/* `promiseCreate` on an id that is taken: read the store, answer 200 with the
   record that is already there, write nothing.  This is Resonate's
   idempotency, and it is the branch the `unique_promise_id` restriction
   defers to. */
rule Server_Promise_Create_Idempotent:
let body = <'promise.create', $S, pid, kind, tgt, grp, pblob2, kblob2>
    resp = <'promise.created', $S, pid, st>
in
    [ In(<body, sig>)
    , !Server($S), !Ltk($S, ~ltkS), !Pk($A, pkA)
    , !PromiseMeta($S, pid, kind0, tgt0, grp0, pblob, kblob, cr)
    , PromiseSt($S, pid, st, vblob) ]
  --[ Eq(verify(sig, body, pkA), true)
    , PromiseCreateNoop($S, pid) ]->
    [ PromiseSt($S, pid, st, vblob)
    , Out(<resp, sign(resp, ~ltkS)>) ]
#endif

#ifdef Core
// Server_Task_Create: omitted under -D=Core -- see the note in section 0
#else
/* `task.create`: a worker announces a promise AND claims its task in one
   step -- the task is born already `acquired`, at version one, held by the
   caller.  (`taskCreate`, external.lean.)  This is the root-invocation path;
   `Server_Promise_Create_Targeted` above is the path that leaves the task on
   offer. */
rule Server_Task_Create:
let body   = <'task.create', $S, pid, $W, tgt, grp, pblob, kblob>
    nver   = vsucc('v0')
    capTok = sign(<'cap', $S, pid, nver, $W>, ~ltkS)
    resp   = <'task.created', $S, pid, nver, $W, pblob, kblob, capTok>
in
    [ In(<body, sig>)
    , !Server($S), !Ltk($S, ~ltkS)
    , !Pk($W, pkW), !Member($W, grp) ]
  --[ Eq(verify(sig, body, pkW), true)
    , PromiseBorn($S, pid)
    , TaskBorn($S, pid, nver)
    , CreatedBy($S, pid, $W)
    , Acquire($S, pid, nver, $W)
    , GrantCap($S, pid, nver, $W)
    , LeaseHeld($S, pid, nver, $W) ]->
    [ !PromiseMeta($S, pid, 'targeted', tgt, grp, pblob, kblob, $W)
    , !ExternalP($S, pid)
    , PromiseSt($S, pid, 'pending', 'novalue')
    , TaskSt($S, pid, 'acquired', nver, $W)
    , ExecSlot($S, pid, 'empty')
    , Out(<resp, sign(resp, ~ltkS)>) ]
#endif

#ifdef Core
// Server_Task_Create_Adopt: omitted under -D=Core -- see the note in section 0
#else
/* `taskCreate` when the id is ALREADY TAKEN and its task is on offer.  The
   specification's second branch:

       else if t.state == .pending then
         let t := { t with state := .acquired, version := t.version + 1, ... }

   Note what is missing: `taskCreate` does not carry a version, so this is an
   acquisition WITHOUT the version check `taskAcquire` performs.  A worker that
   knows only the promise id can take a task off the offer queue without having
   seen the offer.  It is a distinct authorisation path and it is modelled as
   one; the version still increments, so `claim_is_exclusive` and the fencing
   theorem are unaffected -- what changes is who can start, not who can finish.

   `taskCreate` on a fulfilled task answers 200 and writes nothing, and on an
   acquired or suspended one answers 409; neither moves the state. */
rule Server_Task_Create_Adopt:
let body   = <'task.create', $S, pid, $W, tgt, grp, pblob2, kblob2>
    nver   = vsucc(ver)
    capTok = sign(<'cap', $S, pid, nver, $W>, ~ltkS)
    resp   = <'task.created', $S, pid, nver, $W, pblob, kblob, capTok>
in
    [ In(<body, sig>)
    , !Server($S), !Ltk($S, ~ltkS)
    , !Pk($W, pkW), !Member($W, grp)
    , TaskSt($S, pid, 'tpending', ver, 'nobody')
    , PromiseSt($S, pid, 'pending', vblob)
    , !PromiseMeta($S, pid, 'targeted', tgt0, grp, pblob, kblob, cr) ]
  --[ Eq(verify(sig, body, pkW), true)
    , Acquire($S, pid, nver, $W)
    , GrantCap($S, pid, nver, $W)
    , Revoke($S, pid, ver)
    , AdoptedWithoutVersion($S, pid, nver, $W)
    , LeaseHeld($S, pid, nver, $W) ]->
    [ TaskSt($S, pid, 'acquired', nver, $W)
    , PromiseSt($S, pid, 'pending', vblob)
    , Out(<resp, sign(resp, ~ltkS)>) ]
#endif

#ifdef Core
// Worker_Task_Create: omitted under -D=Core -- see the note in section 0
#else
/* The worker side of `task.create`: the worker announces the promise and
   claims its task in one step.  It seals the param to its OWN group, since it
   is both the creator and the first holder. */
rule Worker_Task_Create:
let pblob = senc(<'param', ~param>, ~ek)
    kblob = aenc(<'ek', $pid, ~ek>, pk(~gsk))
    body  = <'task.create', $S, $pid, $W, $tgt, $G, pblob, kblob>
in
    [ Fr(~ek), Fr(~param)
    , !Worker($W, $G, ~gsk), !Ltk($W, ~ltkW), !Server($S) ]
  --[ SecretParam(~param, $pid, $G)
    , Seal(~ek, $pid, $G)
    , ClaimSent($W, $S, $pid, vsucc('v0'))
    , WorkerCreates($W, $pid, $G)
    , OnlyOnce(<'task-create', $W, $pid>) ]->
    [ Out(<body, sign(body, ~ltkW)>)
    , WorkerSelfClaiming($W, $S, $pid, ~ek, ~param) ]
#endif

#ifdef Core
// Schedule_Create: omitted under -D=Core -- see the note in section 0
#else
/* Schedules.  `nextCron`/`occurrences`/`expand` are opaque in the
   specification; the only thing a schedule contributes to the rest of the
   protocol is another birth site for a promise, at an id derived from the
   schedule's template and an occurrence instant.  `expand` is a free function
   symbol here, so occurrence ids are not client-choosable -- which is exactly
   the `expand_ids_are_not_forgeable` assumption the specification flags as
   load-bearing and unproved. */
rule Schedule_Create:
let body = <'schedule.create', $S, $sid, $tgt, $G, pblob, kblob>
in
    [ In(<body, sig>), !Server($S), !Pk($A, pkA) ]
  --[ Eq(verify(sig, body, pkA), true)
    , ScheduleBorn($S, $sid)
    , OnlyOnce(<'schedule', $S, $sid>) ]->
    [ !ScheduleRec($S, $sid, $tgt, $G, pblob, kblob) ]
#endif

#ifdef Core
// Schedule_Fire: omitted under -D=Core -- see the note in section 0
#else
rule Schedule_Fire:
let pid  = expand($sid, ~occ)
    resp = <'promise.created', $S, pid, 'pending', 'novalue'>
in
    [ Fr(~occ)
    , !ScheduleRec($S, $sid, $tgt, $G, pblob, kblob)
    , !Ltk($S, ~ltkS) ]
  --[ PromiseBorn($S, pid)
    , TaskBorn($S, pid, 'v0')
    , ScheduleFired($S, $sid, pid) ]->
    [ !PromiseMeta($S, pid, 'targeted', $tgt, $G, pblob, kblob, $S)
    , !ExternalP($S, pid)
    , PromiseSt($S, pid, 'pending', 'novalue')
    , TaskSt($S, pid, 'tpending', 'v0', 'nobody')
    , ExecSlot($S, pid, 'empty')
    , Out(<resp, sign(resp, ~ltkS)>) ]
#endif


// ============================================================================
// SECTION 3 -- the offer, and the claim
//
//   r6 `processRetryTimeout` re-offers a pending task on the server's own
//   cadence; the offer is the `execute` message, addressed to the promise's
//   `resonate:target`.  A worker answers with `task.acquire`, and a successful
//   claim mints the capability.
// ============================================================================

/* r6, `processRetryTimeout`.  Guards: the task is pending, its dispatch clock
   is due, and its promise is still pending.  Time is abstracted, so "due" is
   unconstrained -- see the header.  The effect is to re-arm the clock and to
   write the `execute` entry into the task's outbox slot, replacing whatever
   was there (`Effect.setMessage` is keyed by task id).

   The offer is signed by the server: a worker must not be made to start work
   by anyone who can put a packet on the wire. */
rule Internal_Retry_Dispatch:
let msg = <'execute', $S, tid, ver>
in
    [ TaskSt($S, tid, 'tpending', ver, 'nobody')
    , PromiseSt($S, tid, 'pending', vblob)
    , !PromiseMeta($S, tid, 'targeted', tgt, grp, pblob, kblob, cr)
    , ExecSlot($S, tid, slot)
    , !Ltk($S, ~ltkS) ]
  --[ Dispatch($S, tid, ver)
    , ServerOffers($S, tid, ver, tgt, grp) ]->
    [ TaskSt($S, tid, 'tpending', ver, 'nobody')
    , PromiseSt($S, tid, 'pending', vblob)
    , ExecSlot($S, tid, <tgt, ver>)
    , Out(<msg, sign(msg, ~ltkS)>) ]

/* A worker sees an offer it can authenticate and answers with a claim.  The
   claim names the worker, so the server can bind the capability to it. */
rule Worker_Claim:
let msg  = <'execute', $S, tid, ver>
    body = <'task.acquire', $S, tid, ver, $W>
in
    [ In(<msg, sigS>)
    , !Worker($W, $G, ~gsk), !Ltk($W, ~ltkW)
    , !Server($S), !Pk($S, pkS) ]
  --[ Eq(verify(sigS, msg, pkS), true)
    , WorkerSawOffer($W, $S, tid, ver)
    , ClaimSent($W, $S, tid, vsucc(ver)) ]->
    [ Out(<body, sign(body, ~ltkW)>)
    , WorkerClaiming($W, $S, tid, ver) ]

/* `taskAcquire`.  Guards, in the specification's order: the task exists; its
   promise exists; the task is pending; the promise is pending; the stored
   version equals the version the claimant quoted.  All five are enforced by
   pattern matching on `TaskSt` / `PromiseSt` -- a claim quoting the wrong
   version simply does not unify, which is the 409.

   Added by the identity model: the claim must be signed by the worker it
   names, and that worker must be a member of the group the promise was sealed
   to.  A principal outside the group could not read the param anyway; refusing
   the claim keeps it from taking the task off the offer queue.

   Effect (`external.lean`): acquired, version incremented, holder recorded,
   dispatch disarmed, resumes cleared -- the version bump is what clears the
   resume ledger here, since `TaskResume` facts are version-tagged.

   The capability is minted here and nowhere else. */
rule Server_Task_Acquire:
let body   = <'task.acquire', $S, tid, ver, $W>
    nver   = vsucc(ver)
    capTok = sign(<'cap', $S, tid, nver, $W>, ~ltkS)
    resp   = <'task.acquired', $S, tid, nver, $W, pblob, kblob, capTok>
in
    [ In(<body, sigW>)
    , !Server($S), !Ltk($S, ~ltkS)
    , !Pk($W, pkW), !Member($W, grp)
    , TaskSt($S, tid, 'tpending', ver, 'nobody')
    , PromiseSt($S, tid, 'pending', vblob)
    , !PromiseMeta($S, tid, 'targeted', tgt, grp, pblob, kblob, cr) ]
  --[ Eq(verify(sigW, body, pkW), true)
    , Acquire($S, tid, nver, $W)
    , GrantCap($S, tid, nver, $W)
    , Revoke($S, tid, ver)
    , LeaseHeld($S, tid, nver, $W) ]->
    [ TaskSt($S, tid, 'acquired', nver, $W)
    , PromiseSt($S, tid, 'pending', vblob)
    , Out(<resp, sign(resp, ~ltkS)>) ]

/* The worker takes delivery of the claim response.  The response is matched
   in the shape it must have if the worker is to make any use of it: the key
   blob must open under the group key to the envelope key that the param blob
   is sealed with.  That is the model of a successful decrypt.

   `!WorkerCap` is the capability as the worker holds it; `WorkerRunning` is
   the worker's execution state, carrying the cleartext param. */
rule Worker_Start:
let pblob  = senc(<'param', param>, ek)
    kblob  = aenc(<'ek', tid, ek>, pk(~gsk))
    capm   = <'cap', $S, tid, nver, $W>
    resp   = <'task.acquired', $S, tid, nver, $W, pblob, kblob, capTok>
in
    [ In(<resp, sigS>)
    , WorkerClaiming($W, $S, tid, ver)
    , !Worker($W, $G, ~gsk)
    , !Pk($S, pkS) ]
  --[ Eq(verify(sigS, resp, pkS), true)
    , Eq(verify(capTok, capm, pkS), true)
    , Eq(nver, vsucc(ver))
    , WorkerHoldsCap($W, $S, tid, nver)
    , Unseal(ek, tid)
    , WorkerReadsParam($W, param, tid) ]->
    [ !WorkerCap($W, $S, tid, nver, capTok)
    , WorkerRunning($W, $S, tid, nver, ek, param) ]


#ifdef Core
// Worker_Start_Own: omitted under -D=Core -- see the note in section 0
#else
/* Delivery of the `task.create` response: same shape as `Worker_Start`. */
rule Worker_Start_Own:
let capm = <'cap', $S, tid, nver, $W>
    resp = <'task.created', $S, tid, nver, $W, senc(<'param', param>, ek),
            aenc(<'ek', tid, ek>, pk(~gsk)), capTok>
in
    [ In(<resp, sigS>)
    , WorkerSelfClaiming($W, $S, tid, ek, param)
    , !Worker($W, $G, ~gsk)
    , !Pk($S, pkS) ]
  --[ Eq(verify(sigS, resp, pkS), true)
    , Eq(verify(capTok, capm, pkS), true)
    , Eq(nver, vsucc('v0'))
    , WorkerHoldsCap($W, $S, tid, nver)
    , WorkerReadsParam($W, param, tid) ]->
    [ !WorkerCap($W, $S, tid, nver, capTok)
    , WorkerRunning($W, $S, tid, nver, ek, param) ]
#endif


// ============================================================================
// SECTION 4 -- what the capability authorises
//
//   task.fence, task.suspend, task.complete, task.release, task.heartbeat.
//   Every one of them carries the capability minted at the claim, and every
//   one of them is signed by the worker the capability names.  The server
//   checks BOTH -- possession of the token and proof that the presenter is
//   the principal the token names -- so a stolen token is inert.
//
//   The specification's own guards are unchanged and are enforced by pattern
//   matching, exactly as in `external.lean`:
//
//       t.state != .acquired  -> 409      (matched: 'acquired')
//       p.state != .pending   -> 409      (matched: 'pending')
//       t.version != version  -> 409      (matched: the same `ver`)
// ============================================================================

// ---- task.complete -- `task.fulfill` on the wire, `taskFulfill` in the
//      specification.  The SDK calls it "complete"; the wire surface and
//      `02-abstract/external.lean` call it "fulfill".  Same operation.

/* The worker seals its result under the SAME envelope key the param arrived
   under, so the value is readable by exactly the principals the param was:
   the group, and the creator who chose the key. */
rule Worker_Complete:
let vblob = senc(<'value', ~value>, ek)
    body  = <'task.fulfill', $S, tid, ver, $W, st, vblob, capTok>
in
    [ WorkerRunning($W, $S, tid, ver, ek, param)
    , !WorkerCap($W, $S, tid, ver, capTok)
    , !Worker($W, $G, ~gsk)
    , !Ltk($W, ~ltkW)
    , In(st)
    , Fr(~value) ]
  --[ Settable(st)
    , SecretValue(~value, tid, ek)
    , WorkerCompletes($W, $S, tid, ver, st) ]->
    [ Out(<body, sign(body, ~ltkW)>) ]

/* `taskFulfill`, plus the `setSettled` it ends with: the promise is settled
   and, because a targeted promise always has a task, that task is fulfilled
   in the same step (`TaskObject.fulfill` -- holder, ttl, lease and dispatch
   all cleared, version kept). */
rule Server_Task_Complete:
let capm = <'cap', $S, tid, ver, $W>
    body = <'task.fulfill', $S, tid, ver, $W, st, vblob, capTok>
    resp = <'task.completed', $S, tid, st>
in
    [ In(<body, sigW>)
    , !Server($S), !Ltk($S, ~ltkS), !Pk($W, pkW)
    , TaskSt($S, tid, 'acquired', ver, $W)
    , PromiseSt($S, tid, 'pending', 'novalue')
    , !PromiseMeta($S, tid, 'targeted', tgt, grp, pblob, kblob, cr) ]
  --[ Eq(verify(sigW, body, pkW), true)
    , Eq(verify(capTok, capm, pk(~ltkS)), true)
    , Settable(st)
    , UsedCap($S, tid, ver, $W, 'complete')
    , CompletedBy($S, tid, ver, $W)
    , Settled($S, tid, st, vblob)
    , TaskFulfilled($S, tid, ver) ]->
    [ TaskSt($S, tid, 'fulfilled', ver, 'nobody')
    , PromiseSt($S, tid, st, vblob)
    , Out(<resp, sign(resp, ~ltkS)>) ]

// ---- task.fence ------------------------------------------------------------

/* `taskFence` with a `create` action.  The fence is what makes a worker's
   writes conditional on it still holding the lease: the inner
   `promise.create` runs only if the task is still acquired at the version the
   worker quotes.  `req.action.targetId == req.id` is a 400 -- a fence may not
   act on its own promise -- which is the `Neq` below.

   The child is created targeted, so it is born with a task of its own and
   goes on offer.  This is how a durable execution spawns a child invocation. */
rule Server_Task_Fence_Create:
let capm = <'cap', $S, tid, ver, $W>
    body = <'task.fence', $S, tid, ver, $W, 'create',
            <cid, 'targeted', ctgt, cgrp, cpblob, ckblob>, capTok>
    resp = <'task.fenced', $S, tid, ver, 'create', cid>
in
    [ In(<body, sigW>)
    , !Server($S), !Ltk($S, ~ltkS), !Pk($W, pkW)
    , TaskSt($S, tid, 'acquired', ver, $W)
    , PromiseSt($S, tid, 'pending', 'novalue')
    , !PromiseMeta($S, tid, 'targeted', tgt, grp, pblob, kblob, cr) ]
  --[ Eq(verify(sigW, body, pkW), true)
    , Eq(verify(capTok, capm, pk(~ltkS)), true)
    , Neq(cid, tid)
    , UsedCap($S, tid, ver, $W, 'fence')
    , FencedCreate($S, tid, ver, $W, cid)
    , PromiseBorn($S, cid)
    , TaskBorn($S, cid, 'v0')
    , CreatedBy($S, cid, $W)
    , LeaseHeld($S, tid, ver, $W) ]->
    [ TaskSt($S, tid, 'acquired', ver, $W)
    , PromiseSt($S, tid, 'pending', 'novalue')
    , !PromiseMeta($S, cid, 'targeted', ctgt, cgrp, cpblob, ckblob, $W)
    , !ExternalP($S, cid)
    , PromiseSt($S, cid, 'pending', 'novalue')
    , TaskSt($S, cid, 'tpending', 'v0', 'nobody')
    , ExecSlot($S, cid, 'empty')
    , Out(<resp, sign(resp, ~ltkS)>) ]

/* The worker's side of a fenced create: it mints a child envelope key, seals
   the child's param to the child's group, and remembers the key so that it
   can read the child's value when it is unblocked. */
rule Worker_Fence_Create:
let cpblob = senc(<'param', ~cparam>, ~cek)
    ckblob = aenc(<'ek', $cid, ~cek>, pkCG)
    body   = <'task.fence', $S, tid, ver, $W, 'create',
              <$cid, 'targeted', $ctgt, $CG, cpblob, ckblob>, capTok>
in
    [ WorkerRunning($W, $S, tid, ver, ek, param)
    , !WorkerCap($W, $S, tid, ver, capTok)
    , !Ltk($W, ~ltkW), !Pk($CG, pkCG)
    , Fr(~cek), Fr(~cparam) ]
  --[ SecretParam(~cparam, $cid, $CG)
    , Seal(~cek, $cid, $CG)
    , WorkerFences($W, $S, tid, ver, $cid)
    , OnlyOnce(<'fence-create', $W, tid, $cid>) ]->
    [ Out(<body, sign(body, ~ltkW)>)
    , WorkerRunning($W, $S, tid, ver, ek, param)
    , WorkerChild($W, $S, tid, $cid, ~cek, ~cparam) ]

/* `taskFence` with a `settle` action: the inner call is `promiseSettle`, run
   under the same fence.  Two rules, because `setSettled` fulfils the target's
   task when the target is targeted and there is no task to fulfil when it is
   not (`consistent_task_iff_targeted_promise`). */
rule Server_Task_Fence_Settle_Untargeted:
let capm = <'cap', $S, tid, ver, $W>
    body = <'task.fence', $S, tid, ver, $W, 'settle', <cid, st, cvblob>, capTok>
    resp = <'task.fenced', $S, tid, ver, 'settle', cid>
in
    [ In(<body, sigW>)
    , !Server($S), !Ltk($S, ~ltkS), !Pk($W, pkW)
    , TaskSt($S, tid, 'acquired', ver, $W)
    , PromiseSt($S, tid, 'pending', 'novalue')
    , PromiseSt($S, cid, 'pending', 'novalue')
    , !PromiseMeta($S, cid, ckind, ctgt, cgrp, cpblob, ckblob, ccr) ]
  --[ Eq(verify(sigW, body, pkW), true)
    , Eq(verify(capTok, capm, pk(~ltkS)), true)
    , Neq(cid, tid)
    , Untargeted(ckind)
    , Settable(st)
    , UsedCap($S, tid, ver, $W, 'fence')
    , FencedSettle($S, tid, ver, $W, cid)
    , Settled($S, cid, st, cvblob)
    , LeaseHeld($S, tid, ver, $W) ]->
    [ TaskSt($S, tid, 'acquired', ver, $W)
    , PromiseSt($S, tid, 'pending', 'novalue')
    , PromiseSt($S, cid, st, cvblob)
    , Out(<resp, sign(resp, ~ltkS)>) ]

rule Server_Task_Fence_Settle_Targeted:
let capm = <'cap', $S, tid, ver, $W>
    body = <'task.fence', $S, tid, ver, $W, 'settle', <cid, st, cvblob>, capTok>
    resp = <'task.fenced', $S, tid, ver, 'settle', cid>
in
    [ In(<body, sigW>)
    , !Server($S), !Ltk($S, ~ltkS), !Pk($W, pkW)
    , TaskSt($S, tid, 'acquired', ver, $W)
    , PromiseSt($S, tid, 'pending', 'novalue')
    , PromiseSt($S, cid, 'pending', 'novalue')
    , TaskSt($S, cid, ctst, cver, cholder)
    , !PromiseMeta($S, cid, 'targeted', ctgt, cgrp, cpblob, ckblob, ccr) ]
  --[ Eq(verify(sigW, body, pkW), true)
    , Eq(verify(capTok, capm, pk(~ltkS)), true)
    , Neq(cid, tid)
    , Settable(st)
    , UsedCap($S, tid, ver, $W, 'fence')
    , FencedSettle($S, tid, ver, $W, cid)
    , Settled($S, cid, st, cvblob)
    , TaskFulfilled($S, cid, cver)
    , SettledTargetedBy($S, cid, $W)
    , LeaseHeld($S, tid, ver, $W) ]->
    [ TaskSt($S, tid, 'acquired', ver, $W)
    , PromiseSt($S, tid, 'pending', 'novalue')
    , PromiseSt($S, cid, st, cvblob)
    , TaskSt($S, cid, 'fulfilled', cver, 'nobody')
    , Out(<resp, sign(resp, ~ltkS)>) ]

// ---- task.release ----------------------------------------------------------

/* `taskRelease`: the holder gives the lease back.  The task returns to pending
   AT THE SAME VERSION, with its dispatch re-armed.  The capability the worker
   holds names that version, but it names it together with the state
   `acquired`, which the task no longer has -- so the released capability
   authorises nothing until someone claims again, and a claim bumps the
   version. */
rule Server_Task_Release:
let capm = <'cap', $S, tid, ver, $W>
    body = <'task.release', $S, tid, ver, $W, capTok>
    resp = <'task.released', $S, tid, ver>
in
    [ In(<body, sigW>)
    , !Server($S), !Ltk($S, ~ltkS), !Pk($W, pkW)
    , TaskSt($S, tid, 'acquired', ver, $W)
    , PromiseSt($S, tid, 'pending', vblob) ]
  --[ Eq(verify(sigW, body, pkW), true)
    , Eq(verify(capTok, capm, pk(~ltkS)), true)
    , UsedCap($S, tid, ver, $W, 'release')
    , Released($S, tid, ver, $W)
    , Revoke($S, tid, ver) ]->
    [ TaskSt($S, tid, 'tpending', ver, 'nobody')
    , PromiseSt($S, tid, 'pending', vblob)
    , Out(<resp, sign(resp, ~ltkS)>) ]

rule Worker_Release:
let body = <'task.release', $S, tid, ver, $W, capTok>
in
    [ WorkerRunning($W, $S, tid, ver, ek, param)
    , !WorkerCap($W, $S, tid, ver, capTok)
    , !Ltk($W, ~ltkW) ]
  --[ WorkerReleases($W, $S, tid, ver) ]->
    [ Out(<body, sign(body, ~ltkW)>) ]

// ---- task.heartbeat --------------------------------------------------------

/* `heartbeatOne`.  This is the one operation the specification already binds
   to an identity: it checks `t.pid == some pid` as well as the version.  Its
   only effect is to push `expiresAt` out, which is a temporal field and so
   carries no value here -- the rule is kept because WHO may refresh a lease is
   a security question even when the refresh itself is invisible. */
rule Server_Task_Heartbeat:
let capm = <'cap', $S, tid, ver, $W>
    body = <'task.heartbeat', $S, tid, ver, $W, capTok>
    resp = <'task.heartbeat.ok', $S, tid, ver>
in
    [ In(<body, sigW>)
    , !Server($S), !Ltk($S, ~ltkS), !Pk($W, pkW)
    , TaskSt($S, tid, 'acquired', ver, $W)
    , PromiseSt($S, tid, 'pending', vblob) ]
  --[ Eq(verify(sigW, body, pkW), true)
    , Eq(verify(capTok, capm, pk(~ltkS)), true)
    , UsedCap($S, tid, ver, $W, 'heartbeat')
    , Heartbeat($S, tid, ver, $W)
    , LeaseHeld($S, tid, ver, $W) ]->
    [ TaskSt($S, tid, 'acquired', ver, $W)
    , PromiseSt($S, tid, 'pending', vblob)
    , Out(<resp, sign(resp, ~ltkS)>) ]

rule Worker_Heartbeat:
let body = <'task.heartbeat', $S, tid, ver, $W, capTok>
in
    [ WorkerRunning($W, $S, tid, ver, ek, param)
    , !WorkerCap($W, $S, tid, ver, capTok)
    , !Ltk($W, ~ltkW) ]
  --[ WorkerHeartbeats($W, $S, tid, ver) ]->
    [ Out(<body, sign(body, ~ltkW)>)
    , WorkerRunning($W, $S, tid, ver, ek, param) ]


// ============================================================================
// SECTION 5 -- obligations: callbacks and listeners
//
//   A callback is an obligation to WAKE A TASK when a promise settles; a
//   listener is an obligation to DELIVER A RECORD to an address.  Both may be
//   registered only on an `external` promise, both are sets, and both are
//   discharged by an internal drain step (r4 and r3).
// ============================================================================

#ifdef Core
// Server_Promise_Register_Callback: omitted under -D=Core -- see the note in section 0
#else
/* `promiseRegisterCallback`, the branch that writes.  Guards, in order:
   awaited != awaiter (400); awaited exists (404); awaiter exists (422);
   awaiter carries `resonate:target` (422); awaited is external (422); awaited
   pending and awaiter pending -- only then is the callback recorded. */
rule Server_Promise_Register_Callback:
let body = <'promise.register_callback', $S, awaited, awaiter>
    resp = <'promise.callback.registered', $S, awaited, awaiter>
in
    [ In(<body, sig>)
    , !Server($S), !Ltk($S, ~ltkS), !Pk($A, pkA)
    , !ExternalP($S, awaited)
    , PromiseSt($S, awaited, 'pending', avblob)
    , PromiseSt($S, awaiter, 'pending', wvblob)
    , !PromiseMeta($S, awaiter, 'targeted', wtgt, wgrp, wpblob, wkblob, wcr) ]
  --[ Eq(verify(sig, body, pkA), true)
    , Neq(awaited, awaiter)
    , CallbackAdded($S, awaited, awaiter)
    , RegisterCallback($S, awaited, awaiter, $A) ]->
    [ PromiseSt($S, awaited, 'pending', avblob)
    , PromiseSt($S, awaiter, 'pending', wvblob)
    , Callback($S, awaited, awaiter)
    , !CallbackReg($S, awaited, awaiter)
    , Out(<resp, sign(resp, ~ltkS)>) ]
#endif

#ifdef Reduced
// Server_Promise_Register_Callback_Duplicate: response-only, omitted under -D=Reduced
#else
/* The idempotent branch: the pair is already registered, so answer 200 and
   write nothing (`PromiseObject.addCallback` returns `p` unchanged). */
rule Server_Promise_Register_Callback_Duplicate:
let body = <'promise.register_callback', $S, awaited, awaiter>
    resp = <'promise.callback.registered', $S, awaited, awaiter>
in
    [ In(<body, sig>)
    , !Server($S), !Ltk($S, ~ltkS), !Pk($A, pkA)
    , !CallbackReg($S, awaited, awaiter) ]
  --[ Eq(verify(sig, body, pkA), true)
    , CallbackNoop($S, awaited, awaiter) ]->
    [ Out(<resp, sign(resp, ~ltkS)>) ]
#endif

#ifdef Reduced
// Server_Promise_Register_Callback_Settled: response-only, omitted under -D=Reduced
#else
/* The settled branch: an awaited promise that has already settled takes no
   callback -- the caller is told the outcome instead.  Writing nothing here is
   what keeps `monotone_promise_callbacks_shrink_once_settled` true. */
rule Server_Promise_Register_Callback_Settled:
let body = <'promise.register_callback', $S, awaited, awaiter>
    resp = <'promise.callback.settled', $S, awaited, st>
in
    [ In(<body, sig>)
    , !Server($S), !Ltk($S, ~ltkS), !Pk($A, pkA)
    , !ExternalP($S, awaited)
    , PromiseSt($S, awaited, st, vblob) ]
  --[ Eq(verify(sig, body, pkA), true)
    , Neq(st, 'pending')
    , CallbackNoop($S, awaited, awaiter) ]->
    [ PromiseSt($S, awaited, st, vblob)
    , Out(<resp, sign(resp, ~ltkS)>) ]
#endif

/* `promiseRegisterListener`, the branch that writes.  `addressValid` (400) is
   modelled by the address being a public name; the awaited promise must be
   external (422) and pending.  Unlike a callback, a listener is registered on
   behalf of an ADDRESS, not a task, so there is no awaiter to check.

   `UnblockSlot` is the outbox slot for this listener -- the outbox is keyed by
   `<promise, address>` for unblocks, so one slot per registered listener. */
rule Server_Promise_Register_Listener:
let body = <'promise.register_listener', $S, awaited, $addr>
    resp = <'promise.listener.registered', $S, awaited, $addr>
in
    [ In(<body, sig>)
    , !Server($S), !Ltk($S, ~ltkS), !Pk($A, pkA)
    , !ExternalP($S, awaited)
    , PromiseSt($S, awaited, 'pending', avblob) ]
  --[ Eq(verify(sig, body, pkA), true)
    , ListenerAdded($S, awaited, $addr)
    , RegisterListener($S, awaited, $addr, $A) ]->
    [ PromiseSt($S, awaited, 'pending', avblob)
    , Listener($S, awaited, $addr)
    , !ListenerReg($S, awaited, $addr)
    , UnblockSlot($S, awaited, $addr, 'empty')
    , Out(<resp, sign(resp, ~ltkS)>) ]

#ifdef Reduced
// Server_Promise_Register_Listener_Duplicate: response-only, omitted under -D=Reduced
#else
rule Server_Promise_Register_Listener_Duplicate:
let body = <'promise.register_listener', $S, awaited, $addr>
    resp = <'promise.listener.registered', $S, awaited, $addr>
in
    [ In(<body, sig>)
    , !Server($S), !Ltk($S, ~ltkS), !Pk($A, pkA)
    , !ListenerReg($S, awaited, $addr) ]
  --[ Eq(verify(sig, body, pkA), true)
    , ListenerNoop($S, awaited, $addr) ]->
    [ Out(<resp, sign(resp, ~ltkS)>) ]
#endif

#ifdef Reduced
// Server_Promise_Register_Listener_Settled: response-only, omitted under -D=Reduced
#else
rule Server_Promise_Register_Listener_Settled:
let body = <'promise.register_listener', $S, awaited, $addr>
    resp = <'promise.listener.settled', $S, awaited, st>
in
    [ In(<body, sig>)
    , !Server($S), !Ltk($S, ~ltkS), !Pk($A, pkA)
    , !ExternalP($S, awaited)
    , PromiseSt($S, awaited, st, vblob) ]
  --[ Eq(verify(sig, body, pkA), true)
    , Neq(st, 'pending')
    , ListenerNoop($S, awaited, $addr) ]->
    [ PromiseSt($S, awaited, st, vblob)
    , Out(<resp, sign(resp, ~ltkS)>) ]
#endif

/* A client registers a listener on a promise it created, at its own address,
   and remembers the envelope key so that it can open the value when the
   `unblock` arrives. */
rule Client_Register_Listener:
let body = <'promise.register_listener', $S, pid, $addr>
in
    [ ClientAwaits($C, $S, pid, ek, param)
    , !Ltk($C, ~ltkC) ]
  --[ ClientListens($C, $S, pid, $addr) ]->
    [ Out(<body, sign(body, ~ltkC)>)
    , ClientListening($C, $S, pid, $addr, ek, param) ]


// ============================================================================
// SECTION 6 -- task.suspend
//
//   The worker parks its task on one or more awaited promises.  Guards
//   (`taskSuspend`, external.lean):
//
//     actions empty                     -> 400
//     any awaited == the task's own id  -> 400
//     duplicate awaited ids             -> 400
//     task missing / promise missing    -> 404 / 409
//     task not acquired                 -> 409
//     task's promise not pending        -> 409
//     version mismatch                  -> 409
//     any awaited missing or not external -> 422
//     ANY awaited already settled       -> 300, resumes cleared, STILL ACQUIRED
//     otherwise                         -> callbacks registered, task suspended
//
//   Arity one and arity two are both given, with every branch.  Higher arities
//   are structurally identical: `checkAwaited` is a fold whose only output is
//   "did any of them settle", and `registerAwaited` is a map.
// ============================================================================

// ---- arity 1, the suspending branch ----------------------------------------

rule Server_Task_Suspend_1:
let capm = <'cap', $S, tid, ver, $W>
    body = <'task.suspend', $S, tid, ver, $W, <a1>, capTok>
    resp = <'task.suspended', $S, tid, ver>
in
    [ In(<body, sigW>)
    , !Server($S), !Ltk($S, ~ltkS), !Pk($W, pkW)
    , TaskSt($S, tid, 'acquired', ver, $W)
    , PromiseSt($S, tid, 'pending', 'novalue')
    , !ExternalP($S, a1)
    , PromiseSt($S, a1, 'pending', v1) ]
  --[ Eq(verify(sigW, body, pkW), true)
    , Eq(verify(capTok, capm, pk(~ltkS)), true)
    , Neq(a1, tid)
    , UsedCap($S, tid, ver, $W, 'suspend')
    , Suspended($S, tid, ver, $W)
    , Revoke($S, tid, ver)
    , CallbackAdded($S, a1, tid)
    , RegisterCallback($S, a1, tid, $W) ]->
    [ TaskSt($S, tid, 'suspended', ver, 'nobody')
    , PromiseSt($S, tid, 'pending', 'novalue')
    , PromiseSt($S, a1, 'pending', v1)
    , Callback($S, a1, tid)
    , !CallbackReg($S, a1, tid)
    , Out(<resp, sign(resp, ~ltkS)>) ]

// ---- arity 1, the 300 branch: the awaited promise has already settled -------

/* 300, not 200.  The task stays ACQUIRED and keeps its lease -- the worker is
   told to carry on rather than to park -- and `resumes` is cleared.  Getting
   this branch wrong is how a runtime parks a task that will never be woken,
   because the settlement that would have woken it has already happened. */
rule Server_Task_Suspend_1_AlreadySettled:
let capm = <'cap', $S, tid, ver, $W>
    body = <'task.suspend', $S, tid, ver, $W, <a1>, capTok>
    resp = <'task.suspend.retry', $S, tid, ver>
in
    [ In(<body, sigW>)
    , !Server($S), !Ltk($S, ~ltkS), !Pk($W, pkW)
    , TaskSt($S, tid, 'acquired', ver, $W)
    , PromiseSt($S, tid, 'pending', 'novalue')
    , !ExternalP($S, a1)
    , PromiseSt($S, a1, st1, v1) ]
  --[ Eq(verify(sigW, body, pkW), true)
    , Eq(verify(capTok, capm, pk(~ltkS)), true)
    , Neq(a1, tid)
    , Neq(st1, 'pending')
    , UsedCap($S, tid, ver, $W, 'suspend')
    , SuspendRefused($S, tid, ver, $W)
    , LeaseHeld($S, tid, ver, $W) ]->
    [ TaskSt($S, tid, 'acquired', ver, $W)
    , PromiseSt($S, tid, 'pending', 'novalue')
    , PromiseSt($S, a1, st1, v1)
    , Out(<resp, sign(resp, ~ltkS)>) ]

// ---- arity 2 ---------------------------------------------------------------

#ifdef Core
// Server_Task_Suspend_2: omitted under -D=Core -- see the note in section 0
#else
rule Server_Task_Suspend_2:
let capm = <'cap', $S, tid, ver, $W>
    body = <'task.suspend', $S, tid, ver, $W, <a1, a2>, capTok>
    resp = <'task.suspended', $S, tid, ver>
in
    [ In(<body, sigW>)
    , !Server($S), !Ltk($S, ~ltkS), !Pk($W, pkW)
    , TaskSt($S, tid, 'acquired', ver, $W)
    , PromiseSt($S, tid, 'pending', 'novalue')
    , !ExternalP($S, a1), !ExternalP($S, a2)
    , PromiseSt($S, a1, 'pending', v1)
    , PromiseSt($S, a2, 'pending', v2) ]
  --[ Eq(verify(sigW, body, pkW), true)
    , Eq(verify(capTok, capm, pk(~ltkS)), true)
    , Neq(a1, tid), Neq(a2, tid), Neq(a1, a2)
    , UsedCap($S, tid, ver, $W, 'suspend')
    , Suspended($S, tid, ver, $W)
    , Revoke($S, tid, ver)
    , CallbackAdded($S, a1, tid)
    , CallbackAdded($S, a2, tid)
    , RegisterCallback($S, a1, tid, $W)
    , RegisterCallback($S, a2, tid, $W) ]->
    [ TaskSt($S, tid, 'suspended', ver, 'nobody')
    , PromiseSt($S, tid, 'pending', 'novalue')
    , PromiseSt($S, a1, 'pending', v1)
    , PromiseSt($S, a2, 'pending', v2)
    , Callback($S, a1, tid), !CallbackReg($S, a1, tid)
    , Callback($S, a2, tid), !CallbackReg($S, a2, tid)
    , Out(<resp, sign(resp, ~ltkS)>) ]
#endif

#ifdef Core
// Server_Task_Suspend_2_AlreadySettled: omitted under -D=Core -- see the note in section 0
#else
/* `checkAwaited` folds over the whole list: if ANY awaited promise has
   settled, the answer is 300 and NOTHING is registered -- not even for the
   awaited promises that are still pending.  That all-or-nothing is the
   atomicity the specification's `some true` branch encodes, and it is why
   this rule cannot be decomposed into two arity-one suspends. */
rule Server_Task_Suspend_2_AlreadySettled:
let capm = <'cap', $S, tid, ver, $W>
    body = <'task.suspend', $S, tid, ver, $W, <a1, a2>, capTok>
    resp = <'task.suspend.retry', $S, tid, ver>
in
    [ In(<body, sigW>)
    , !Server($S), !Ltk($S, ~ltkS), !Pk($W, pkW)
    , TaskSt($S, tid, 'acquired', ver, $W)
    , PromiseSt($S, tid, 'pending', 'novalue')
    , !ExternalP($S, a1), !ExternalP($S, a2)
    , PromiseSt($S, a1, st1, v1)
    , PromiseSt($S, a2, st2, v2) ]
  --[ Eq(verify(sigW, body, pkW), true)
    , Eq(verify(capTok, capm, pk(~ltkS)), true)
    , Neq(a1, tid), Neq(a2, tid), Neq(a1, a2)
    , Neq(st1, 'pending')
    , UsedCap($S, tid, ver, $W, 'suspend')
    , SuspendRefused($S, tid, ver, $W)
    , LeaseHeld($S, tid, ver, $W) ]->
    [ TaskSt($S, tid, 'acquired', ver, $W)
    , PromiseSt($S, tid, 'pending', 'novalue')
    , PromiseSt($S, a1, st1, v1)
    , PromiseSt($S, a2, st2, v2)
    , Out(<resp, sign(resp, ~ltkS)>) ]
#endif

/* The worker's side.  A worker that suspends gives up its execution state:
   the task is parked on the server, and when it is woken it goes back on
   offer and may be claimed by a DIFFERENT worker of the group.  That is why
   the param has to be readable by the group and not by one worker. */
rule Worker_Suspend_1:
let body = <'task.suspend', $S, tid, ver, $W, <$a1>, capTok>
in
    [ WorkerRunning($W, $S, tid, ver, ek, param)
    , !WorkerCap($W, $S, tid, ver, capTok)
    , !Ltk($W, ~ltkW) ]
  --[ WorkerSuspends($W, $S, tid, ver, $a1) ]->
    [ Out(<body, sign(body, ~ltkW)>) ]

#ifdef Core
// Worker_Suspend_2: omitted under -D=Core -- see the note in section 0
#else
rule Worker_Suspend_2:
let body = <'task.suspend', $S, tid, ver, $W, <$a1, $a2>, capTok>
in
    [ WorkerRunning($W, $S, tid, ver, ek, param)
    , !WorkerCap($W, $S, tid, ver, capTok)
    , !Ltk($W, ~ltkW) ]
  --[ WorkerSuspends($W, $S, tid, ver, $a1) ]->
    [ Out(<body, sign(body, ~ltkW)>) ]
#endif


// ============================================================================
// SECTION 7 -- promise.settle, promise.get, task.halt, task.continue
//
//   These are the operations the specification leaves UNGATED -- no version,
//   no lease, no identity.  They are modelled exactly as they are, and the
//   consequences are the subject of lemmas 12 and 13 below.
// ============================================================================

/* `promiseSettle`.  The specification checks `settable` and that the promise
   is pending.  It does NOT check who is asking.  Any principal that knows a
   promise id may settle it.  For an external promise -- a webhook, a human
   approval -- that IS the design.  For a TARGETED promise it means a
   settlement can land while a worker holds the lease, which is what
   `Server_Promise_Settle_Targeted` below models.

   Compile with `-D=FencedSettle` to remove that rule and gate targeted
   promises behind the fence instead. */
rule Server_Promise_Settle_Untargeted:
let body = <'promise.settle', $S, pid, st, vblob>
    resp = <'promise.settled', $S, pid, st>
in
    [ In(<body, sig>)
    , !Server($S), !Ltk($S, ~ltkS), !Pk($A, pkA)
    , PromiseSt($S, pid, 'pending', 'novalue')
    , !PromiseMeta($S, pid, kind, tgt, grp, pblob, kblob, cr) ]
  --[ Eq(verify(sig, body, pkA), true)
    , Untargeted(kind)
    , Settable(st)
    , Settled($S, pid, st, vblob)
    , SettledBy($S, pid, $A) ]->
    [ PromiseSt($S, pid, st, vblob)
    , Out(<resp, sign(resp, ~ltkS)>) ]

#ifdef FencedSettle
// `Server_Promise_Settle_Targeted` is removed under -D=FencedSettle: a targeted
// promise may then be settled only through the capability-gated task API.
#else
rule Server_Promise_Settle_Targeted:
let body = <'promise.settle', $S, pid, st, vblob>
    resp = <'promise.settled', $S, pid, st>
in
    [ In(<body, sig>)
    , !Server($S), !Ltk($S, ~ltkS), !Pk($A, pkA)
    , PromiseSt($S, pid, 'pending', 'novalue')
    , TaskSt($S, pid, tst, tver, holder)
    , !PromiseMeta($S, pid, 'targeted', tgt, grp, pblob, kblob, cr) ]
  --[ Eq(verify(sig, body, pkA), true)
    , Settable(st)
    , Settled($S, pid, st, vblob)
    , SettledBy($S, pid, $A)
    , SettledTargetedBy($S, pid, $A)
    , TaskFulfilled($S, pid, tver) ]->
    [ PromiseSt($S, pid, st, vblob)
    , TaskSt($S, pid, 'fulfilled', tver, 'nobody')
    , Out(<resp, sign(resp, ~ltkS)>) ]
#endif

#ifdef Reduced
// Server_Promise_Settle_Noop: response-only, omitted under -D=Reduced
#else
/* The already-settled branch: 200 with the record as it stands, no write.
   This is what makes settlement idempotent and one-way. */
rule Server_Promise_Settle_Noop:
let body = <'promise.settle', $S, pid, st2, vblob2>
    resp = <'promise.settled', $S, pid, st>
in
    [ In(<body, sig>)
    , !Server($S), !Ltk($S, ~ltkS), !Pk($A, pkA)
    , PromiseSt($S, pid, st, vblob) ]
  --[ Eq(verify(sig, body, pkA), true)
    , Neq(st, 'pending')
    , Settable(st2)
    , SettleNoop($S, pid) ]->
    [ PromiseSt($S, pid, st, vblob)
    , Out(<resp, sign(resp, ~ltkS)>) ]
#endif

#ifdef Reduced
// Server_Promise_Get: response-only, omitted under -D=Reduced
#else
/* `promiseGet`.  The read channel: it hands back the stored record, blobs and
   all.  It is ungated, so it is the cheapest way for anyone who knows an id
   to obtain `pblob` and `kblob`.  Modelling it is what makes the secrecy
   lemmas mean something -- they have to hold even though the ciphertexts are
   free for the asking. */
rule Server_Promise_Get:
let body = <'promise.get', $S, pid>
    resp = <'promise.record', $S, pid, st, kind, tgt, grp, pblob, kblob, vblob>
in
    [ In(body)
    , !Server($S), !Ltk($S, ~ltkS)
    , PromiseSt($S, pid, st, vblob)
    , !PromiseMeta($S, pid, kind, tgt, grp, pblob, kblob, cr) ]
  --[ PromiseRead($S, pid) ]->
    [ PromiseSt($S, pid, st, vblob)
    , Out(<resp, sign(resp, ~ltkS)>) ]
#endif

#ifdef Reduced
// Server_Task_Get: response-only, omitted under -D=Reduced
#else
/* `taskGet`. */
rule Server_Task_Get:
let body = <'task.get', $S, tid>
    resp = <'task.record', $S, tid, tst, ver, holder>
in
    [ In(body)
    , !Server($S), !Ltk($S, ~ltkS)
    , TaskSt($S, tid, tst, ver, holder) ]
  --[ TaskRead($S, tid) ]->
    [ TaskSt($S, tid, tst, ver, holder)
    , Out(<resp, sign(resp, ~ltkS)>) ]
#endif

/* `taskHalt`.  Any state but `fulfilled` may be halted; there is no version
   and no lease check in the specification, so any principal may halt any
   task.  Halting clears the holder -- it revokes a live lease. */
rule Server_Task_Halt:
let body = <'task.halt', $S, tid>
    resp = <'task.halted', $S, tid>
in
    [ In(<body, sig>)
    , !Server($S), !Ltk($S, ~ltkS), !Pk($A, pkA)
    , TaskSt($S, tid, tst, ver, holder)
    , PromiseSt($S, tid, 'pending', vblob) ]
  --[ Eq(verify(sig, body, pkA), true)
    , Neq(tst, 'fulfilled')
    , Neq(tst, 'halted')
    , Halted($S, tid, ver, $A)
    , Revoke($S, tid, ver) ]->
    [ TaskSt($S, tid, 'halted', ver, 'nobody')
    , PromiseSt($S, tid, 'pending', vblob)
    , Out(<resp, sign(resp, ~ltkS)>) ]

/* `taskContinue`: halted back to pending, dispatch re-armed, version kept. */
rule Server_Task_Continue:
let body = <'task.continue', $S, tid>
    resp = <'task.continued', $S, tid>
in
    [ In(<body, sig>)
    , !Server($S), !Ltk($S, ~ltkS), !Pk($A, pkA)
    , TaskSt($S, tid, 'halted', ver, 'nobody')
    , PromiseSt($S, tid, 'pending', vblob) ]
  --[ Eq(verify(sig, body, pkA), true)
    , Continued($S, tid, ver, $A) ]->
    [ TaskSt($S, tid, 'tpending', ver, 'nobody')
    , PromiseSt($S, tid, 'pending', vblob)
    , Out(<resp, sign(resp, ~ltkS)>) ]


// ============================================================================
// SECTION 8 -- the internal steps
//
//   r1 the promise timeout, r3 the listener drain, r4 the callback drain,
//   r5 the lease timeout.  (r6, the retry dispatch, is in section 3 where the
//   offer it produces is.)  Nobody asks for these; the server takes them on
//   its own initiative, and they take the FORCED reads -- they materialise
//   under both readings of the machine.
// ============================================================================

#ifdef Core
// Internal_Promise_Timeout_Timer: omitted under -D=Core -- see the note in section 0
#else
/* r1, `processPromiseTimeout` = `touchPromise`, whose whole content is
   `PromiseObject.project`: a pending promise past its deadline settles, as
   `resolved` if it is a timer and as `rejectedTimedout` otherwise, WITH NO
   VALUE.  `rejectedTimedout` is server-owned -- `PromiseState.settable`
   refuses it to clients -- so these three rules are the only writers of it.

   A timer is never targeted (`Tags.timerTargeted` is refused at every birth
   door), so the timer case has no task to answer for. */
rule Internal_Promise_Timeout_Timer:
    [ PromiseSt($S, pid, 'pending', 'novalue')
    , !PromiseMeta($S, pid, 'timer', 'none', grp, pblob, kblob, cr) ]
  --[ TimedOut($S, pid, 'resolved')
    , Settled($S, pid, 'resolved', 'novalue') ]->
    [ PromiseSt($S, pid, 'resolved', 'novalue') ]
#endif

rule Internal_Promise_Timeout_Untargeted:
    [ PromiseSt($S, pid, 'pending', 'novalue')
    , !PromiseMeta($S, pid, kind, 'none', grp, pblob, kblob, cr) ]
  --[ Untargeted(kind)
    , Neq(kind, 'timer')
    , TimedOut($S, pid, 'rejectedTimedout')
    , Settled($S, pid, 'rejectedTimedout', 'novalue') ]->
    [ PromiseSt($S, pid, 'rejectedTimedout', 'novalue') ]

/* `setSettled`: settling a promise that has a task fulfils that task in the
   same step.  TIMEOUT ALWAYS WINS -- the lease does not protect a promise
   from its own deadline, and the holder's capability dies with it. */
rule Internal_Promise_Timeout_Targeted:
    [ PromiseSt($S, pid, 'pending', 'novalue')
    , TaskSt($S, pid, tst, ver, holder)
    , !PromiseMeta($S, pid, 'targeted', tgt, grp, pblob, kblob, cr) ]
  --[ Neq(tst, 'fulfilled')
    , TimedOut($S, pid, 'rejectedTimedout')
    , Settled($S, pid, 'rejectedTimedout', 'novalue')
    , TaskFulfilled($S, pid, ver)
    , Revoke($S, pid, ver) ]->
    [ PromiseSt($S, pid, 'rejectedTimedout', 'novalue')
    , TaskSt($S, pid, 'fulfilled', ver, 'nobody') ]

/* r3, `processListener`.  The promise has settled and this address is still
   on its listener list: discharge the obligation and write the `unblock` into
   the outbox slot for that <promise, address> pair.  The obligation is
   consumed, which is `monotone_promise_listeners_shrink_once_settled`.

   The message carries the promise RECORD -- id, state, value -- and the value
   is still the sealed blob.  The server is the courier, not a reader. */
rule Internal_Listener_Drain:
let msg = <'unblock', $S, pid, st, vblob>
in
    [ PromiseSt($S, pid, st, vblob)
    , Listener($S, pid, $addr)
    , UnblockSlot($S, pid, $addr, slot)
    , !Ltk($S, ~ltkS) ]
  --[ Neq(st, 'pending')
    , UnblockSent($S, pid, $addr, st, vblob) ]->
    [ PromiseSt($S, pid, st, vblob)
    , UnblockSlot($S, pid, $addr, msg)
    , Out(<msg, sign(msg, ~ltkS)>) ]

/* The listening client takes delivery and opens the value with the envelope
   key it chose at creation.  This is the end of the round trip: a value
   produced by a worker, carried by a server that cannot read it, opened by
   the client that asked for the work. */
rule Client_Receive_Unblock:
let vblob = senc(<'value', value>, ek)
    msg   = <'unblock', $S, pid, st, vblob>
in
    [ In(<msg, sigS>)
    , ClientListening($C, $S, pid, $addr, ek, param)
    , !Pk($S, pkS) ]
  --[ Eq(verify(sigS, msg, pkS), true)
    , Neq(st, 'pending')
    , ClientReadsValue($C, value, pid)
    , ClientUnblocked($C, $S, pid, st) ]->
    [ ClientDone($C, $S, pid, st, value) ]

/* r4, `processCallback` -> `resumeOne`, the branch that WAKES: the awaiter's
   task is suspended, so it goes back to pending with the awaited id recorded
   as the reason and its dispatch armed.  `resumes := [awaited]` -- a single
   element, replacing, which is sound because a suspended task holds none
   (`well_formed_task_suspended_has_no_resumes`). */
rule Internal_Callback_Drain_Resume:
    [ PromiseSt($S, awaited, st, vblob)
    , Callback($S, awaited, awaiter)
    , TaskSt($S, awaiter, 'suspended', ver, 'nobody')
    , PromiseSt($S, awaiter, 'pending', wvblob) ]
  --[ Neq(st, 'pending')
    , CallbackDrained($S, awaited, awaiter)
    , ResumeAdded($S, awaiter, ver, awaited)
    , Resumed($S, awaiter, ver, awaited) ]->
    [ PromiseSt($S, awaited, st, vblob)
    , PromiseSt($S, awaiter, 'pending', wvblob)
    , TaskSt($S, awaiter, 'tpending', ver, 'nobody')
    , TaskResume($S, awaiter, ver, awaited) ]

/* `resumeOne`, the branch that BUFFERS: the awaiter is live but not parked --
   pending, acquired or halted -- so the wake is recorded and nothing moves.
   The worker will see it when it next runs. */
rule Internal_Callback_Drain_Buffer:
    [ PromiseSt($S, awaited, st, vblob)
    , Callback($S, awaited, awaiter)
    , TaskSt($S, awaiter, tst, ver, holder)
    , PromiseSt($S, awaiter, 'pending', wvblob) ]
  --[ Neq(st, 'pending')
    , Neq(tst, 'suspended')
    , Neq(tst, 'fulfilled')
    , CallbackDrained($S, awaited, awaiter)
    , ResumeAdded($S, awaiter, ver, awaited)
    , Buffered($S, awaiter, ver, awaited) ]->
    [ PromiseSt($S, awaited, st, vblob)
    , PromiseSt($S, awaiter, 'pending', wvblob)
    , TaskSt($S, awaiter, tst, ver, holder)
    , TaskResume($S, awaiter, ver, awaited) ]

/* `resumeOne`, the branch that DROPS: the awaiter has already settled, so its
   task is fulfilled and there is nothing to wake.  The obligation is still
   discharged -- `processCallback` removes the callback before it calls
   `resumeOne`. */
rule Internal_Callback_Drain_Fulfilled:
    [ PromiseSt($S, awaited, st, vblob)
    , Callback($S, awaited, awaiter)
    , TaskSt($S, awaiter, 'fulfilled', ver, 'nobody') ]
  --[ Neq(st, 'pending')
    , CallbackDrained($S, awaited, awaiter)
    , ResumeDropped($S, awaiter, awaited) ]->
    [ PromiseSt($S, awaited, st, vblob)
    , TaskSt($S, awaiter, 'fulfilled', ver, 'nobody') ]

/* r5, `processLeaseTimeout`.  The holder's lease has run out while its
   promise is still pending: the task goes back on offer AT THE SAME VERSION,
   with the holder cleared and the dispatch armed.

   This is the crux of the fencing argument.  The version is NOT bumped here,
   so a capability naming `<tid, ver, W>` still names a real version -- but it
   names it together with the state `acquired`, which the task has just lost.
   The capability is inert until someone claims again, and a claim bumps the
   version, so the old holder can never write again.  Lemma
   `no_write_after_lease_loss` is that statement. */
rule Internal_Lease_Timeout:
    [ TaskSt($S, tid, 'acquired', ver, $W)
    , PromiseSt($S, tid, 'pending', vblob) ]
  --[ LeaseLost($S, tid, ver, $W)
    , Revoke($S, tid, ver) ]->
    [ TaskSt($S, tid, 'tpending', ver, 'nobody')
    , PromiseSt($S, tid, 'pending', vblob) ]

/* An `unblock` for a promise that settled at its deadline carries no value
   (`well_formed_promise_deadline_settlement_has_no_value`), so there is
   nothing to open.  The client still learns the outcome. */
rule Client_Receive_Unblock_NoValue:
let msg = <'unblock', $S, pid, st, 'novalue'>
in
    [ In(<msg, sigS>)
    , ClientListening($C, $S, pid, $addr, ek, param)
    , !Pk($S, pkS) ]
  --[ Eq(verify(sigS, msg, pkS), true)
    , Neq(st, 'pending')
    , ClientUnblocked($C, $S, pid, st) ]->
    [ ClientDone($C, $S, pid, st, 'novalue') ]


// ============================================================================
// SECTION 9 -- what is claimed
//
//   Four families:
//
//     A  EXECUTABILITY  -- the model runs; the lemmas below are not vacuous.
//     B  CONFIDENTIALITY -- params and values are readable by the worker group
//        and the creator, and by nobody else, INCLUDING A COMPROMISED SERVER.
//     C  CAPABILITY     -- the claim is what authorises; the capability is
//        bound to one worker and one version, and dies with the lease.
//     D  INTEGRITY      -- offers and unblocks are authentic; a promise settles
//        at most once.
//
//   Then one more, at the end, which is deliberately falsifiable and
//   documents a property the specification does NOT have -- see
//   `attack_open_settle_preempts_the_holder`.
// ============================================================================

// ---- sources ---------------------------------------------------------------

/* The server stores a promise's `pblob` and `kblob` as OPAQUE TERMS.  It takes
 * them off the wire, keeps them, and hands them back on `promise.get`, on the
 * `task.acquire` response and on the `unblock`; it never looks inside, which
 * is the whole point of the design.  The consequence for the prover is that
 * it cannot trace, from the shape of the rules alone, where the cleartext a
 * worker decrypts came from: the chain runs In (on `promise.create`) ->
 * !PromiseMeta -> Out (on the claim response) -> In -> decryption, with an
 * unconstrained variable in the middle.  Those are the open chains, and
 * without these three lemmas the precomputation does not converge.
 *
 * Each says the only thing there is to say about an opaque passthrough: what
 * comes out of one either came from the principal that sealed it, or the
 * adversary already had it.  They are proved by induction over the trace,
 * before anything else, and are then available to the rest.
 *
 * The load-bearing one is the FIRST.  The deconstruction that actually opens
 * the chains is not the payload but the ENVELOPE KEY: `Worker_Start` recovers
 * `ek` by opening `aenc(<'ek', tid, ek>, pk(gsk))`, and that blob is the
 * variable that travelled through the server.  `Seal` is raised wherever an
 * envelope key is minted; `Unseal` in the one rule that opens an envelope it
 * did not seal.
 */
lemma sources_envelope [sources]:
  "All ek tid #i.
       Unseal(ek, tid) @ #i
   ==> (Ex #j. KU(ek) @ #j & #j < #i)
     | (Ex pid G #j. Seal(ek, pid, G) @ #j & #j < #i)"

lemma sources_param [sources]:
  "All W param tid #i.
       WorkerReadsParam(W, param, tid) @ #i
   ==> (Ex #j. KU(param) @ #j & #j < #i)
     | (Ex pid G #j. SecretParam(param, pid, G) @ #j & #j < #i)"

lemma sources_value [sources]:
  "All C value pid #i.
       ClientReadsValue(C, value, pid) @ #i
   ==> (Ex #j. KU(value) @ #j & #j < #i)
     | (Ex tid ek #j. SecretValue(value, tid, ek) @ #j & #j < #i)"

// ---- helper invariants, reused by the rest ---------------------------------

/* An acquired task in the store was put there by a claim.  `LeaseHeld` is
   raised by every rule that PUTS one back -- the claim itself, and the fence,
   the heartbeat and the refused suspend, which take the lease and return it
   unchanged.  Stated this way the induction is local: each step asks only
   where the lease it is holding came from, and the answer is either "this
   claim" or "an earlier holding", which the hypothesis covers.

   NOT `[reuse]`, and that is deliberate.  A `reuse` lemma is saturated into
   every constraint system the prover builds, for every later lemma, and this
   one quantifies over four variables and a timepoint.  Measured on
   `resonate-capability.spthy`: with `[reuse]` on it, even `grant_is_a_claim`
   -- two steps, decidable by looking at the rules -- does not finish in 900 s;
   without it, five seconds.  The heavy inductive lemma is proved and then left
   alone; the light consequence below is what gets carried forward. */
lemma lease_was_granted [use_induction]:
  "All S tid ver W #i.
       LeaseHeld(S, tid, ver, W) @ #i
   ==> Ex #j. GrantCap(S, tid, ver, W) @ #j & (#j < #i | #j = #i)"

/* Everything that presents a capability was granted one -- the light
   consequence, and the hypothesis the capability lemmas are built on.  This
   one DOES need `[reuse]`: dropping it costs `capability_use_requires_claim`
   its proof. */
lemma cap_use_follows_grant [reuse]:
  "All S tid ver W op #i.
       UsedCap(S, tid, ver, W, op) @ #i
   ==> Ex #j. GrantCap(S, tid, ver, W) @ #j & #j < #i"

/* A capability is granted only by a claim, and a claim records the same
   worker and the same version. */
lemma grant_is_a_claim [reuse]:
  "All S tid ver W #i.
       GrantCap(S, tid, ver, W) @ #i ==> Acquire(S, tid, ver, W) @ #i"

/* ONE HOLDER PER VERSION.  Two workers never hold the same <task, version>:
   a claim consumes the pending task and puts back an acquired one at a
   strictly larger version term, so a version is claimed once and once only.
   This is what makes the version a fencing token rather than a counter, and
   it is what `capability_is_bound_to_its_worker` turns into exclusivity. */
lemma claim_is_exclusive [reuse, use_induction]:
  "All S tid ver W1 W2 #i #j.
       Acquire(S, tid, ver, W1) @ #i & Acquire(S, tid, ver, W2) @ #j
   ==> #i = #j"

// ---- A. executability ------------------------------------------------------

/* The whole round trip, with nobody compromised: a client creates a targeted
   promise and listens on it; the server offers the task; a worker claims it,
   opens the param, and completes it with a sealed value; the server delivers
   the unblock; the client opens the value.  If this fails, every lemma below
   is about an empty set of traces. */
lemma exec_full_cycle:
  exists-trace
  "Ex C W S G pid ver param value #c #a #r #k #u.
        ClientCreates(C, pid, G) @ #c
      & Acquire(S, pid, ver, W) @ #a
      & WorkerReadsParam(W, param, pid) @ #r
      & CompletedBy(S, pid, ver, W) @ #k
      & ClientReadsValue(C, value, pid) @ #u
      & #c < #a & #a < #r & #r < #k & #k < #u
      & not(Ex A #z. LtkReveal(A) @ #z)
      & not(Ex GG #z. GroupReveal(GG) @ #z)"

/* The same round trip with the SERVER FULLY COMPROMISED, and the param still
   secret.  This is the non-vacuity check for lemma 5: it shows there are runs
   in which the server's key is in the adversary's hands, work still gets done,
   and the payload still does not leak. */
lemma exec_cycle_under_compromised_server:
  exists-trace
  "Ex C W S G pid ver param value #c #a #r #v #k #z.
        ClientCreates(C, pid, G) @ #c
      & SecretParam(param, pid, G) @ #c
      & Acquire(S, pid, ver, W) @ #a
      & WorkerReadsParam(W, param, pid) @ #r
      & SecretValue(value, pid, G) @ #v
      & CompletedBy(S, pid, ver, W) @ #k
      & #r < #v & #v < #k
      & LtkReveal(S) @ #z
      & not(Ex #q. K(param) @ #q)
      & not(Ex #q. K(value) @ #q)
      & not(Ex GG #q. GroupReveal(GG) @ #q)"

/* Park and wake: a worker suspends its task on an external promise, that
   promise settles, the callback drains, and the task goes back on offer with
   the wake recorded.  This is the branch a durable execution spends most of
   its life in. */
lemma exec_suspend_and_resume:
  exists-trace
  "Ex S W tid ver awaited #a #s #d #w.
        Acquire(S, tid, ver, W) @ #a
      & Suspended(S, tid, ver, W) @ #s
      & CallbackDrained(S, awaited, tid) @ #d
      & Resumed(S, tid, ver, awaited) @ #w
      & #a < #s & #s < #d
      & not(Ex A #z. LtkReveal(A) @ #z)"

/* A fenced child creation: a worker holding a lease spawns a child
   invocation, and the child goes on offer in its own right. */
lemma exec_fenced_create:
  exists-trace
  "Ex S W tid ver cid #a #f #o.
        Acquire(S, tid, ver, W) @ #a
      & FencedCreate(S, tid, ver, W, cid) @ #f
      & Dispatch(S, cid, 'v0') @ #o
      & #a < #f & #f < #o
      & not(Ex A #z. LtkReveal(A) @ #z)"

/* A worker that lost its lease is not the one that finishes the work: the
   task is re-offered and a second worker claims it at a higher version. */
lemma exec_lease_handover:
  exists-trace
  "Ex S W1 W2 tid v1 v2 #a1 #l #a2.
        Acquire(S, tid, v1, W1) @ #a1
      & LeaseLost(S, tid, v1, W1) @ #l
      & Acquire(S, tid, v2, W2) @ #a2
      & #a1 < #l & #l < #a2
      & not(W1 = W2)
      & not(Ex A #z. LtkReveal(A) @ #z)"

// ---- B. confidentiality ----------------------------------------------------

/* THE SERVER CANNOT READ A PARAM.
 *
 * Note what is NOT excluded: `LtkReveal(S)`.  The adversary may hold the
 * server's long-term key, and it already sees every blob the server stores,
 * because every one of them crosses the public network.  Its knowledge is a
 * superset of the server's.  So this lemma says precisely that no amount of
 * server-side access opens a param.  The only exclusion is compromise of the
 * worker group -- either the group key itself (`Compromise_Group`) or a
 * member holding it (`Compromise_Worker`), both of which raise
 * `GroupReveal`. */
lemma param_secrecy:
  "All param pid G #i #j.
       SecretParam(param, pid, G) @ #i & K(param) @ #j
   ==> Ex #r. GroupReveal(G) @ #r"

/* THE SERVER CANNOT READ A VALUE.  Same argument, for the result the worker
   seals under the envelope key the param arrived under. */
lemma value_secrecy:
  "All value tid ek G #i #j #s.
       SecretValue(value, tid, ek) @ #i
     & Seal(ek, tid, G) @ #s
     & K(value) @ #j
   ==> Ex #r. GroupReveal(G) @ #r"

/* Only a member of the group a promise was sealed to ever holds its param in
   clear.  Confidentiality restated as an access-control statement rather than
   an adversary-knowledge one. */
lemma param_read_only_by_group_member:
  "All W param pid G #i #c.
       WorkerReadsParam(W, param, pid) @ #i
     & SecretParam(param, pid, G) @ #c
   ==> (Ex #m. NewWorker(W, G) @ #m)
     | (Ex #r. GroupReveal(G) @ #r)"

// ---- C. capability ---------------------------------------------------------

/* THE CLAIM IS WHAT AUTHORISES.  Every use of the capability -- fence,
   suspend, complete, release, heartbeat -- was preceded by a successful
   `task.acquire` by that same worker at that same version.  There is no
   other way in, and this holds with no compromise assumption at all: even an
   adversary holding the server's signing key, who can mint a syntactically
   valid token, cannot make the server act on it, because the server also
   checks its own record of who holds the lease. */
lemma capability_use_requires_claim [use_induction]:
  "All S tid ver W op #i.
       UsedCap(S, tid, ver, W, op) @ #i
   ==> Ex #j. Acquire(S, tid, ver, W) @ #j & #j < #i"

/* THE CAPABILITY IS NOT A BEARER TOKEN.  If W1 was granted the capability for
   <tid, ver> then no OTHER principal can use it -- stealing the token off the
   wire is not enough, because the request must also be signed by the worker
   the token names. */
lemma capability_is_bound_to_its_worker:
  "All S tid ver W1 W2 op #i #j.
       GrantCap(S, tid, ver, W1) @ #i & UsedCap(S, tid, ver, W2, op) @ #j
   ==> W1 = W2"

/* NO WRITE AFTER LEASE LOSS -- the fencing theorem.
 *
 * `Revoke(S, tid, ver)` is raised wherever version `ver` stops being live: a
 * lease timeout, a release, a suspension, a halt, a deadline settlement, and
 * the next claim.  After any of them, the capability naming that version
 * authorises nothing.  A worker that was partitioned away and comes back with
 * a stale capability cannot complete, cannot fence and cannot suspend. */
lemma no_write_after_lease_loss [use_induction]:
  "All S tid ver W op #i #j #k.
       Acquire(S, tid, ver, W) @ #i
     & Revoke(S, tid, ver) @ #j
     & UsedCap(S, tid, ver, W, op) @ #k
     & #i < #j & #j < #k
   ==> F"

/* A completion is by the worker that claimed, at the version it claimed. */
lemma completion_is_by_the_claimant:
  "All S tid ver W1 W2 #i #j.
       Acquire(S, tid, ver, W1) @ #i & CompletedBy(S, tid, ver, W2) @ #j
   ==> W1 = W2"

/* A worker's capability came from the server, not from the network. */
lemma no_forged_capability:
  "All W S tid ver #i.
       WorkerHoldsCap(W, S, tid, ver) @ #i
   ==> (Ex #j. GrantCap(S, tid, ver, W) @ #j & #j < #i)
     | (Ex #r. LtkReveal(S) @ #r)"

/* The server records as holder only a worker that actually asked -- by
   `task.acquire`, or by the `task.create` adoption path, which is
   `AdoptedWithoutVersion`. */
#ifdef Core
lemma claim_agreement:
  "All S tid ver W #i.
       Acquire(S, tid, ver, W) @ #i
   ==> (Ex #j. ClaimSent(W, S, tid, ver) @ #j & #j < #i)
     | (Ex #r. LtkReveal(W) @ #r)"
#else
lemma claim_agreement:
  "All S tid ver W #i.
       Acquire(S, tid, ver, W) @ #i
   ==> (Ex #j. ClaimSent(W, S, tid, ver) @ #j & #j < #i)
     | (Ex #j. AdoptedWithoutVersion(S, tid, ver, W) @ #j)
     | (Ex #r. LtkReveal(W) @ #r)"
#endif

// ---- D. integrity ----------------------------------------------------------

/* A promise settles once and stays settled -- there is no second settlement
   to disagree with the first.  `preserved_promise_settlement_is_one_way` and
   `preserved_promise_state_frozen_once_settled` in the specification's
   catalogue, as one statement. */
lemma settlement_is_unique:
  "All S pid st1 vb1 st2 vb2 #i #j.
       Settled(S, pid, st1, vb1) @ #i & Settled(S, pid, st2, vb2) @ #j
   ==> #i = #j"

/* A worker starts work only on a task the server actually offered.  Without
   the signature on the `execute` message, anyone able to put a packet on the
   wire could make a worker burn a claim on a task that does not exist. */
lemma offer_authentication:
  "All W S tid ver #i.
       WorkerSawOffer(W, S, tid, ver) @ #i
   ==> (Ex tgt grp #j. ServerOffers(S, tid, ver, tgt, grp) @ #j & #j < #i)
     | (Ex #r. LtkReveal(S) @ #r)"

/* An `unblock` a client acts on was really sent by the server, for a promise
   that had really settled. */
lemma unblock_authentication:
  "All C S pid st #i.
       ClientUnblocked(C, S, pid, st) @ #i
   ==> (Ex addr vb #j. UnblockSent(S, pid, addr, st, vb) @ #j & #j < #i)
     | (Ex #r. LtkReveal(S) @ #r)"

/* A value a client accepts as the answer to its promise was produced by a
   worker that held the lease on that promise's task -- or the promise settled
   without one, through the open `promise.settle` path or its own deadline. */
lemma value_provenance:
  "All C value pid #i.
       ClientReadsValue(C, value, pid) @ #i
   ==> (Ex S W ver #j. CompletedBy(S, pid, ver, W) @ #j & #j < #i)
     | (Ex S A #j. SettledBy(S, pid, A) @ #j & #j < #i)
     | (Ex GG #r. GroupReveal(GG) @ #r)
     | (Ex A #r. LtkReveal(A) @ #r)"

// ---- 15. the property the specification does NOT have ----------------------

/* `promiseSettle` in `02-abstract/external.lean` checks `settable` and that
 * the promise is pending.  It does not check a version, a lease or an
 * identity.  So for a TARGETED promise -- one a worker holds a lease on --
 * any principal that knows the id can settle it out from under that worker,
 * and `setSettled` will fulfil the task in the same step.  The capability
 * model of this theory does not close that door, because the door is not on
 * the task API.
 *
 * This lemma is EXPECTED TO BE FOUND, and finding it is the point: it is an
 * exists-trace, so Tamarin returns the attack as a witness.  Nobody is
 * compromised in it.
 *
 * Compile with `-D=FencedSettle` to drop `Server_Promise_Settle_Targeted`,
 * and this lemma becomes false while `settlement_of_targeted_is_fenced`
 * below becomes true.  That pair is the statement of the fix. */
#ifdef FencedSettle
/* With the open path removed, a targeted promise is settled only by the
 * worker holding its lease, or by the parent invocation fencing a settle on
 * it -- both of which are capability-gated.  The deadline path is not
 * `SettledTargetedBy`; it is the server acting on its own clock, and
 * `PromiseState.settable` keeps a client from forging it. */
lemma settlement_of_targeted_is_fenced:
  "All S pid A #i.
       SettledTargetedBy(S, pid, A) @ #i
   ==> (Ex ver #j. Acquire(S, pid, ver, A) @ #j & #j < #i)
     | (Ex ptid pver #j. FencedSettle(S, ptid, pver, A, pid) @ #j)"
#else
lemma attack_open_settle_preempts_the_holder:
  exists-trace
  "Ex S W A tid ver #a #s.
        Acquire(S, tid, ver, W) @ #a
      & SettledTargetedBy(S, tid, A) @ #s
      & #a < #s
      & not(A = W)
      & not(Ex B #z. LtkReveal(B) @ #z)
      & not(Ex GG #z. GroupReveal(GG) @ #z)"
#endif

end
